---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseStudySlideshow from "../../components/CaseStudySlideshow.astro";
import onectPoster from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_thumbnail_image.jpg?url";
import heroVideo from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Onect_project_main.mp4?url";
import problemImage from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Problem.jpg?url";
import onectImage3 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/onect_image3.jpg?url";
import researchDiagram1 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/ResearchDiagram1.jpg?url";
import researchDiagram2 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/ResearchDiagram2.jpg?url";
import frame75 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Frame 75.jpg?url";
import frame76 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Frame 76.jpg?url";
import portfolioPng from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/portfolio.png?url";
import portfolioJpg from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/portfolio 2.jpg?url";
import eligibilityVideo from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Eligibility Test.mp4?url";
import aiCursorVideo from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/AI Cursor.mp4?url";
import fileScanningVideo from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/File Scanning.mp4?url";
import reviewFilesVideo from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/Review Files.mp4?url";
import ds2 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/DS2.jpg?url";
import ds3 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/DS3.png?url";
import ds4 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/DS4.jpg?url";
import colorPalette from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/color palette.png?url";
import onectBrand from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/onect.jpg?url";
import wireframe1 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/1.jpg?url";
import wireframe2 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/2.jpg?url";
import wireframe3 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/3.jpg?url";
import wireframe4 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/4.jpg?url";
import wireframe5 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/5.jpg?url";
import competitor1 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/competitor1.jpg?url";
import competitor2 from "../../../Resource/Work/Onect/Onect_Detailed/competitor2.jpg?url";

const wireframeSlides = [
  { src: wireframe1, alt: "Wireframe iteration 1" },
  { src: wireframe2, alt: "Wireframe iteration 2" },
  { src: wireframe3, alt: "Wireframe iteration 3" },
  { src: wireframe4, alt: "Wireframe iteration 4" },
  { src: wireframe5, alt: "Wireframe iteration 5" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Untangling one of the most document-heavy visa processes in the U.S."
  brand="Onect AI"
  accent="var(--color-onect)"
  eyebrow="Onect AI / AI-native legal-tech platform + SaaS"
  description="An AI-native platform for the U.S. O-1 visa process, serving applicants and attorneys on one shared system."
  role="Product designer, AI workflow architecture"
  timeline="August 2025 – March 2026 (8 months)"
  team="1 product designer, 2 motion designers"
  skills={["Design system", "User research", "UX/UI"]}
  image={onectPoster}
  video={heroVideo}
  anchors={[
    { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
    { id: "problem", label: "Problem" },
    { id: "research", label: "Research" },
    { id: "solution", label: "Solution" },
    { id: "design-system", label: "Design System" },
  ]}
>
  <div slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      The O-1 visa process breaks down on both ends. Applicants have no clear
      roadmap to prove their extraordinary ability, and attorneys are buried in
      manual document review, where errors are inevitable. Nearly 30% of
      petitions get flagged as a result.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      Onect bridges that gap, giving professionals the structure to build a
      compelling case and attorneys the clarity to review one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-stack">
    <div class="info-card">
      <h4>※ What is an O-1 visa?</h4>
      <p>
        The O-1 is a U.S. work visa for individuals with extraordinary ability,
        requiring 100+ pages of evidence and up to a year of preparation before a
        decision is even made.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="media-stack">
      <figure>
        <img src={onectImage3} alt="Onect product overview" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="impact" data-impact>
      <div class="impact-col impact-col--results">
        <p class="impact-label">Achievement</p>
        <div class="impact-award" data-impact-item>
          <span class="impact-award-mark">Nominated</span>
          <p>UX Design Awards 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="impact-stats">
          <article class="impact-stat" data-impact-item>
            <p class="impact-stat-value">
              <span data-count-to="70">0</span><span class="impact-stat-unit">%</span>
            </p>
            <p class="impact-stat-desc">
              Faster document filing — from 1m 28s down to 26s
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="impact-stat" data-impact-item>
            <p class="impact-stat-value">
              <span data-count-to="80">0</span><span class="impact-stat-unit">%</span>
            </p>
            <p class="impact-stat-desc">
              Better legal-document comprehension through the AI cursor
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="impact-col impact-col--contrib">
        <p class="impact-label">My contribution</p>
        <ul class="impact-list">
          <li data-impact-item>
            <strong>User interviews</strong>
            <span
              >Facilitated 3 interview sessions with lawyers and created affinity
              maps</span
            >
          </li>
          <li data-impact-item>
            <strong>Design system</strong>
            <span
              >Built a consistent design system that applies across the
              platform</span
            >
          </li>
          <li data-impact-item>
            <strong>Interface design</strong>
            <span
              >Designed pages for AI cursor, AI feedback, eligibility tests and
              results, and profile</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">00 — Project Origin</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>Where the problem became personal</h3>
    <div>
      <p>
        The anxiety international colleagues carried through the O-1 process had
        little to do with qualifications. It came from the experience itself: no
        feedback, no structure, and no clear way to know whether the evidence
        they had assembled told a convincing story.
      </p>
      <p>
        This wasn't a legal problem. It was a design problem. Nobody had built
        something that served both the applicant trying to understand their own
        case and the attorney trying to evaluate it efficiently.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-body">
          <p class="callout-title">Design Challenge</p>
          <p>
            How might we redesign the O-1 visa experience so that applicants feel
            confident and in control, while helping attorneys focus on what they
            do best, without disrupting the workflows they already rely on?
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label" id="problem">01 — Problem</p>
  <div class="split problem-split">
    <div class="problem-lead">
      <h3>
        Problem:
        <br />
        Two user types.
        <br />
        <span class="mark">Completely opposing needs.</span>
      </h3>
      <blockquote class="quote-bubble">
        <p>
          “Everyone said hire a lawyer and you'll figure it out, but that wasn't
          true.”
        </p>
        <svg class="quote-bubble-caret" width="28" height="14" viewBox="0 0 28 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M4 0h16L12 14 4 0Z" fill="#f5f5f5" />
        </svg>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-body">
      <div class="user-block">
        <p class="user-label">User 1: Applicant</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Applicants</strong> are left feeling powerless by a lack of
          communication from their lawyers and deeply frustrated by an inability
          to track their own progress.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="user-block">
        <p class="user-label">User 2: Attorney</p>
        <p>
          <strong>On the attorney side</strong>, the problem is inverted: too many
          clients, too little structure. Attorneys spend the majority of their
          intake time reviewing documents that don't meet the threshold —
          documents that an earlier filter could have caught.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src={problemImage} alt="Applicant and attorney tension" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-body">
          <p class="callout-title">Why this matters beyond the users</p>
          <p>
            When the visa process is too overwhelming, talented people give up
            before they even try. Onect doesn't just help individuals file a visa
            petition. It keeps exceptional people moving toward the places where
            they can do their best work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">02 — Goals &amp; Constraints</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>Goals and real-world limits</h3>
    <div>
      <div class="label-block">
        <p><span class="mark-label">Product Goals</span></p>
        <ul class="checklist checklist--goals">
          <li>
            Give applicants real-time visibility into their case progress and
            clear next steps, so they never feel lost
          </li>
          <li>
            Let applicants self-assess their eligibility before committing to
            legal costs, while giving attorneys a way to filter qualified leads
            from the start
          </li>
          <li>
            Reduce attorney intake friction through intelligent document
            classification and proactive error detection
          </li>
          <li>
            Design a system where AI accelerates the process but humans remain
            accountable at every decision point
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="label-block">
        <p>
          <span class="mark-label mark-label--constraint"
            >Constraints We Designed Around</span
          >
        </p>
        <ul class="checklist checklist--constraints">
          <li>
            Legal sensitivity: The product cannot give legal advice. Every
            AI-generated output needed to be clearly framed as guidance, not
            counsel
          </li>
          <li>
            Low willingness to switch: Attorneys already have document workflows.
            Any tool that required a full behavioral change would face immediate
            rejection
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label" id="research">03 — Research &amp; Insights</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>7 conversations.<br />One clear direction</h3>
    <div class="content-div">
      <p class="prose-label">Method</p>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag">SME Interviews (n=7)</span>
        <span class="tag">Empathy Mapping</span>
        <span class="tag">Affinity Mapping</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        We spoke with 4 applicants who had recently received their O-1 visa and
        3 immigration attorneys specializing in O-1 cases. Findings were
        synthesized via empathy mapping, then reframed using Jobs-to-be-Done to
        separate surface complaints from root causes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-stack">
    <div class="media-stack">
      <figure>
        <img src={researchDiagram1} alt="Research synthesis diagram" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={researchDiagram2} alt="Affinity mapping diagram" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="split">
      <div class="sticky-board">
        <h3>Key Jobs-to-be-Done</h3>
        <div class="sticky-notes">
          <article class="sticky-note sticky-note--cream" tabindex="0" style="--rot: -2.2deg">
            <p>
              “Organization and structure could've saved me from weeks of stress.”
            </p>
            <p class="sticky-note-attr">— Applicant B</p>
          </article>
          <article class="sticky-note sticky-note--mint" tabindex="0" style="--rot: 1.6deg">
            <p>
              “My lawyer was dismissive and annoyed when I asked questions, but I
              had to do a lot myself.”
            </p>
            <p class="sticky-note-attr">— Applicant C</p>
          </article>
          <article class="sticky-note sticky-note--blush" tabindex="0" style="--rot: -1.4deg">
            <p>
              “A trustworthy platform must pre-qualify applicants accurately.”
            </p>
            <p class="sticky-note-attr">— Attorney A</p>
          </article>
          <article class="sticky-note sticky-note--lilac" tabindex="0" style="--rot: 2deg">
            <p>
              “We focus heavily on preventing RFEs (Request for Evidence) by having
              the highest quality of work.”
            </p>
            <p class="sticky-note-attr">— Attorney A</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="label-block">
          <p class="prose-label">Applicants' Jobs to Be Done</p>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>
              When my case is in progress, I want real-time visibility into where
              things stand so I never have to chase my attorney for updates.
            </li>
            <li>
              When I don't understand a legal requirement, I want an immediate
              answer so I'm not stuck waiting days for a simple response.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="label-block">
          <p class="prose-label">Lawyers' Jobs to Be Done</p>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>
              When evaluating a new client, I want to quickly assess their
              eligibility upfront so I don't waste hours on cases that won't
              qualify.
            </li>
            <li>
              When reviewing a petition, I want documents automatically flagged for
              errors so I can focus on legal strategy, not manual paperwork.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-stack competitor-analysis">
      <div class="split">
        <h3>The process is discretionary against the very people it serves</h3>
        <div class="content-div">
          <p class="prose-label">Method</p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Competitor Analysis</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            N=7 was a small sample by research standards. So, we triangulated
            findings against public USCIS adjudication data and supplemented with a
            competitor audit of 8 existing tools to increase confidence in the
            patterns identified.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="media-pair">
        <figure>
          <img src={competitor1} alt="Competitor audit overview" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img src={competitor2} alt="Competitor audit comparison" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="split">
        <div></div>
        <p>
          Existing tools (immigration SaaS, attorney portals, USCIS guides) shared a
          common failure: they were built around the process, not the person. None
          offered a dual-sided experience. Most existing immigration tools either
          served attorneys only or treated applicants as passive document suppliers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">04 — Ideation &amp; Direction</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>Designing for two users who want completely different things</h3>
    <div class="content-div">
      <p>
        With two users pulling in opposite directions, our first ideation
        challenge was structural: should this be one product with dual views, or
        two separate products sharing a back-end?
      </p>
      <p>
        The wrong answer here would create either a product that felt incoherent
        to both users, or two products that couldn't deliver the network value of
        a shared data layer.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-body">
          <p class="callout-title">Design Principle Established Here</p>
          <p>
            We could have automated more. But in legal processes, one wrong
            assumption can derail someone's entire case. So every AI action
            required a human to sign off, not because technology is infeasible, but
            because trust isn't something you can automate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <CaseStudySlideshow slides={wireframeSlides} />

  <p class="section-label" id="solution">05 — Solution</p>
  <div class="solution-set">
    <div class="split">
      <h3>
        Solution: A system that{" "}
        <span class="mark">serves two users without compromising either</span>
      </h3>
      <p>
        Onect AI is a role-based platform where applicants and attorneys share a
        data layer but never share a view. The applicant experience is built around
        clarity and momentum. The attorney experience is built around efficiency
        without disruption.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="media-block">
      <video src={reviewFilesVideo} autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div class="feature-meta">
      <p class="feature-label">Feature 01 — <span>Applicant</span></p>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <h3>Eligibility Check + Smart Matching</h3>
        <p>
          Applicants begin with an eligibility test, immediately understanding
          where they stand for the first time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="media-block">
      <video src={eligibilityVideo} muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div class="feature-meta">
      <p class="feature-label">Feature 02 — <span>Applicant</span></p>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <h3>AI Document Assistant</h3>
        <p>
          This was the research-backed decision to replace traditional legal
          gatekeeping with affordable, instant clarity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="media-block">
      <video src={aiCursorVideo} muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div class="feature-meta">
      <p class="feature-label">Feature 03 — <span>Attorney</span></p>
      <div class="feature-copy">
        <h3>Intelligent Document Classification</h3>
        <p>
          This single feature addresses the root cause of the 100+ page review
          problem: not the volume, but the absence of any intelligent pre-sort.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="media-block">
      <video src={fileScanningVideo} muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label" id="design-system">06 — Design system</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>Building credibility through restraint</h3>
    <p>
      The visual language of Onect AI needed to communicate two things
      simultaneously: intelligence (this is a sophisticated AI system) and
      trustworthiness (this is handling your most sensitive documents).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="media-stack">
    <figure>
      <img src={onectBrand} alt="Onect brand mark" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={ds2} alt="Onect design system overview" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure>
        <img src={frame75} alt="Onect design system components" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={frame76} alt="Onect design system details" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src={ds4} alt="Onect design system screen" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure>
        <img src={portfolioPng} alt="Onect design system UI" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={portfolioJpg} alt="Onect design system UI detail" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="media-pair">
      <figure>
        <img src={colorPalette} alt="Onect color palette" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={ds3} alt="Onect design system UI detail" loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">Reflection</p>
  <div class="split">
    <h3>What I learned and what I'd do differently</h3>
    <div>
      <p>
        Designing for trust is a distinct skill from designing for usability. In
        high-stakes contexts, a confusing UI that feels safe will outperform a
        slick UI that feels opaque. Transparency in AI reasoning wasn't a
        nice-to-have; it was a requirement.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next phase of this work involves end-to-end PII encryption
        architecture, a financial model that doesn't require applicants to pay
        attorney rates for AI-assisted guidance, and a data layer that gets more
        accurate as more petitions flow through it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
